window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-163825ded2be6d83"]={"key":"ny_b1-163825ded2be6d83","group":"ny_b1","uid":"163825ded2be6d83","rank":72,"title":"litigation reserve memo childrens apps 2024","occupation":"lawyers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are the lawyer at Meadowgate Advisory advising Saltmarsh Energy on the children’s-app portfolio it acquired two years ago, and Bianca Sandoval needs your annual litigation reserve view before the auditors arrive on 13 January 2025. Her 2023 memorandum booked $1.07 million by applying 10% to portfolio revenue, and she expects the same shortcut to be rolled forward. Juno Andersen, who leads the product unit, uses “reserve” to mean plausible cash exposure rather than the amount that belongs in the accounts, so the two readers are asking the same word to do different jobs. Nothing has been asserted as of 31 December 2024: there is no demand, lawsuit, regulator contact or announced investigation, but the latest internal review found a changed risk profile. SparkSprout, directed to children under 13, had 430,000 child accounts and $4.8 million revenue in 2024 versus 310,000 and $3.6 million in 2023; retrievable parental-consent evidence is absent for 18% of current accounts versus 6% last year. GridQuest, a mixed-audience app with behavioural advertising, had 1.1 million users and $7.2 million revenue versus 900,000 and $6.0 million, with 9% versus 7% self-declaring as under 13 and no separate consent flow for that group. WattWatch Club, sold through schools without advertising, had 210,000 child profiles and $1.4 million revenue versus 170,000 and $1.1 million; its contracts say schools are authorised to provide consent. Prepare `saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx`, no more than five pages, comparing 2024 with 2023 and giving Bianca one recommended amount for the accounts plus a defensible exposure range. Ground the range in publicly reported regulatory resolutions and settled private actions involving comparable children’s digital products from 1 January 2021 through 31 December 2024. Published figures often mix headline penalties, suspended portions, refunds, settlement funds and per-user calculations, while the largest names are not similar in scale. Make clear what you mean by reserve, draw the line between what the accounting conclusion requires and what prudent planning supports, and say when the prior revenue shortcut ceases to be useful without inventing client or matter figures. Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly that filename.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx` — Decision memorandum to counsel comparing the two periods, assessing the litigation-reserve treatment, and recommending a recorded amount with a separately reasoned exposure range. (A valid DOCX of no more than five rendered pages, placed at the top level of the working directory under the exact filename. It must use an information cutoff of 31 December 2024 and must not invent client facts or public-matter amounts.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx","bytes":42426,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/63cfe7a7442ad0e954244999d7870e735ef50626dd1ec058650cf517c9cb0a8c.docx","sha256":"63cfe7a7442ad0e954244999d7870e735ef50626dd1ec058650cf517c9cb0a8c","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"DECISION MEMORANDUM\nSaltmarsh Energy - children's-app portfolio litigation reserve\nTo\nBianca Sandoval, CFO; Juno Andersen, Product Unit\nFrom\nMeadowgate Advisory - legal counsel\nDate\n8 January 2025 (information cutoff: 31 December 2024)\nPurpose\nYear-end recognition, disclosure and planning view before auditors arrive 13 January 2025\nDecision in one minute\nRecord $0 for this unasserted matter at 31 December 2024. Maintain a separate gross planning exposure range of $0.5 million to $3.0 million (penalties, refunds/redress and settlement value; before insurance, indemnity, tax or deductibility, and excluding unpriced compliance work). The range is a scenario bracket, not a prediction or a required balance.\nI use “reserve” here to mean the accounting loss-contingency balance. Juno’s use - plausible cash exposure - is useful for planning but must be called exposure, not the booked reserve. The two numbers should not be netted or substituted for one another.\nFacts and year-over-year change\nProduct\n2024\n2023\nChange\nRisk reading\nSparkSprout\n430k accts; $4.8m\n310k; $3.6m\n+39%; +33%\nChild-directed (\u003c13). Retrievable parental-consent evidence absent for 18% of current accounts vs 6%.\nGridQuest\n1.1m users; $7.2m\n900k; $6.0m\n+22%; +20%\nMixed audience; behavioural advertising. Under-13 self-declaration 9% vs 7%; no separate consent flow.\nWattWatch Club\n210k profiles; $1.4m\n170k; $1.1m\n+24%; +27%\nSchool-sold, no advertising. Contracts state schools are authorised to provide consent.\nPortfolio\n13.4m revenue\n10.7m revenue\n+25%\n2023 shortcut: 10% x $10.7m = $1.07m booked.\nThe change is not simply scale. SparkSprout’s missing evidence triples; GridQuest combines a growing under-13 segment with behavioural advertising and no separate flow; WattWatch’s school-consent contract is a materially different fact pattern. User totals, self-declared children, child profiles and accounts lacking retrievable evidence are not interchangeable denominators.\nAccounting conclusion (what must follow)\nUnder the applicable loss-contingency model, accrual requires (1) an unfavourable outcome that is probable under the relevant threshold and (2) a reasonably estimable amount. For an unasserted claim, likelihood of assertion is considered before outcome. At cutoff there is no demand, lawsuit, regulator contact or announced investigation. Internal control findings increase potential magnitude and uncertainty, but are not an asserted matter. A calculable planning bracket therefore does not compel an accrual.\nRecommendation: book exactly $0.00 at 31 December 2024. This is one accounting decision, not a conclusion that the products are compliant or that loss is remote. Finance and counsel should assess disclosure separately: if the likelihood of a claim is reasonably possible and material, describe the nature and an estimate or range (or explain why one cannot be made); do not imply that non-accrual ends the analysis. Reassess on a demand, regulator contact, investigation notice or reliable settlement posture.\nPlanning exposure (what should be maintained)\nPlanning scope is gross monetary resolution value for privacy/consent conduct, before unknown insurance, indemnification, tax or deductibility. It excludes the unpriced cost of deletion, monitoring, engineering and compliance obligations, which may be operationally significant but are not assigned invented dollars. The bracket is deliberately rounded to avoid false precision and is not a per-user tariff, adjudicated damages figure or guaranteed settlement value.\nLow case - $0.5m: a modest app-publisher resolution with limited affected population, cooperation and targeted redress. High case - $3.0m: overlapping penalty/redress or a negotiated private fund across the portfolio, with SparkSprout and GridQuest driving the result. WattWatch is a lower-severity comparator on the supplied facts; no amount is added for its school contracts beyond the qualitative uncertainty they reduce.\nDrivers to update the bracket are assertion posture, duration/knowledge, monetisation (especially behavioural advertising), quality of consent evidence, remediation and cooperation, ability to pay, and enterprise scale. Probability and magnitude are separate: a severe possible outcome is not thereby probable.\nComparable outcomes (1 Jan 2021 - 31 Dec 2024)\nMatter / date\nMonetary components\nWhy it informs / limits\nSource anchor\nFTC v WW International (Kurbo), 2022\n$1.5m civil penalty; deletion/algorithmic remedies\nChild-directed health app; close conduct fit. Consent order is negotiated, not damages precedent.\nFTC, “WW International to Pay $1.5 Million,” 3 Oct 2022.\nFTC v Epic Games, 2022\n$275m civil penalty; separate $245m refunds\nChildren’s gaming and dark-pattern conduct. Refund pool is not penalty; Epic scale is an outlier, used only as ceiling context.\nFTC, “Epic Games Will Pay $520 Million,” 19 Dec 2022.\nFTC v Microsoft/Xbox, 2023\n$20m civil penalty\nChild-account retention/consent resolution; platform scale requires downward qualitative adjustment.\nFTC, “Microsoft to Pay $20 Million,” 5 Jun 2023.\nFTC v Edmodo, 2023\n$6m announced civil penalty, payment suspended for inability to pay\nEducation platform; suspended amount is not ordinary cash paid and is not used as a cash midpoint.\nFTC, “Edmodo Will Pay $6 Million,” 22 May 2023.\nFTC v NGL Labs, 2024\n$5m headline: approx. $4.5m consumer redress + $0.5m civil penalty\nChild/teen messaging; redress and penalty remain separate; useful for remedy taxonomy, not a blended rate.\nFTC, “NGL to Pay $5 Million,” 9 Jul 2024.\nFTC v Tilting Point, 2024\n$0.5m civil penalty; nonmonetary compliance relief\nChildren’s game publisher, closer monetary scale; injunctive terms have no invented cash value.\nFTC, “Tilting Point to Pay $500,000,” 18 Jun 2024.\nSelection criteria: U.S. public outcomes in the window involving children’s digital products and privacy/consent conduct, with a stated resolution posture, remedy type and enough scale context to avoid a raw average. Device/platform and very large-enterprise matters are analogues or outliers, not same-scale app comparables. Publicly announced private class actions in this window did not provide a sufficiently verified, final, same-conduct settlement fund for this portfolio; allegations, verdicts and proposed settlements are not filled in as “comparables.”\nDo not add components twice: Epic’s $520m headline is split into $275m penalty and $245m refunds; NGL’s $5m is split into redress and penalty; Edmodo’s $6m is suspended. Consent orders and settlements evidence negotiated resolution, not binding per-user rates. No per-user transfer is used here because denominators differ (users, child accounts, profiles and consent-gap accounts).\nWhy the 10% shortcut stops working here\nThe 2023 method was arithmetically clear: $3.6m + $6.0m + $1.1m = $10.7m; 10% = $1.07m. Applied mechanically to 2024, it would be $13.4m x 10% = $1.34m. That rule of thumb ceases to be useful when claim posture, remedy type, affected-child denominator, duration/knowledge, advertising conduct or remediation is not correlated with revenue. Here, no matter is asserted and risk changed unevenly by product; revenue growth is therefore a poor proxy for either assertion probability or loss magnitude. It should not be rolled forward.\nActions before the 13 January audit visit\nBianca/Finance: retain the $0 booking decision and this gross range as separate workpapers; decide disclosure wording with counsel before 13 January. Juno/Product: by 10 January, validate the 18% SparkSprout consent-evidence gap, document the GridQuest under-13 population and implement/describe a separate consent flow. Counsel: by 13 January, refresh assertion indicators, preserve relevant records and confirm whether any regulator, demand or claimant contact changes the unasserted-matter conclusion. Recalculate only from verified denominators; do not net unknown insurance or indemnity.\nFinal decision\nAs of 31 December 2024, Saltmarsh should record $0.00 for this unasserted children’s-app matter. Maintain, separately for prudent planning and remediation, a gross exposure range of $0.5m-$3.0m under the scope and limitations above; revisit recognition and disclosure if assertion or investigation occurs.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required DOCX exists at the top level under the exact filename `saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx`.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file path and extension.","grader_hint":"Confirm exact spelling, extension and top-level placement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submission is a valid, readable DOCX whose text and principal tables render without corruption or clipping.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"DOCX package inspection and rendered document.","grader_hint":"Open and render the document; ordinary line wrapping is acceptable, but missing content, broken objects or unreadable tables are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memorandum is no more than five pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render using a standard office suite and count all pages, including appendices and endnotes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum is decision-oriented for counsel, the CFO and auditors rather than a generic privacy-law survey.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Framing, prioritisation and treatment of the requested accounting decision.","grader_hint":"Award when a reader can quickly identify the decision, basis and implications without extracting them from a case digest."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum states an information cutoff of 31 December 2024 and identifies the comparable-outcome window as 1 January 2021 through 31 December 2024.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dating or scope language in the memorandum.","grader_hint":"Equivalent unambiguous date formulations qualify."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Client-specific facts are confined to the supplied portfolio facts, with any analytical assumptions expressly labelled rather than presented as discovered facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Factual narrative, calculations and qualifications.","grader_hint":"Check especially for invented complaint counts, investigation probabilities, insurance terms, claim histories or remediation costs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The prior-period discussion correctly reports the 2023 booked amount as $1.07 million and relates it to 10% of the $10.7 million 2023 portfolio revenue.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Prior-period comparison and supporting calculation.","grader_hint":"Accept rounding consistent with $3.6m + $6.0m + $1.1m = $10.7m and 10% = $1.07m."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The current-period analysis addresses all three products and their materially different audience, consent and advertising characteristics.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Product-level risk analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for distinct treatment of a child-directed app with missing consent evidence, a mixed-audience ad-supported app without a separate consent flow, and a school-mediated no-advertising app."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Portfolio revenue totals and period changes are arithmetically correct: 2024 revenue equals the sum of the three 2024 product amounts and 2023 revenue equals the corresponding 2023 sum.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Revenue comparison or calculation.","grader_hint":"The correct totals are derivable as $13.4m for 2024 and $10.7m for 2023; minor display rounding is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum explains the material year-over-year risk changes rather than merely listing the two periods.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparative analysis of growth, consent-evidence deterioration, under-13 mix and product differences.","grader_hint":"A strong comparison connects the changes to exposure or uncertainty and notes that not all user growth carries equal legal significance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum deliberately chooses and states a primary meaning of “reserve,” while acknowledging the alternative accounting-booking and planning-exposure usage.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Defined terminology and organisation of the conclusion.","grader_hint":"Either primary reading is acceptable if explicit and consistently maintained."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A booked loss contingency is kept conceptually distinct from gross or planning exposure; the document does not imply that every plausible exposure must be accrued.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Doctrinal explanation and treatment of the two numerical outputs.","grader_hint":"Look for two clearly labelled concepts and no conflation in the recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum makes exactly one clear recommendation for the amount to record in the accounts as of 31 December 2024.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive conclusion and final decision statement.","grader_hint":"A point amount is required; alternatives may be analysed, but the final booking recommendation cannot remain as a menu."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommended recorded amount is identical wherever it appears and agrees with the memorandum’s stated recognition analysis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary, body, tables and conclusion.","grader_hint":"Trace the number across the document and check that its treatment follows the author’s stated probability and estimability conclusions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exposure range has a defined low and high value, uses one consistent scope, and repeats consistently wherever summarised.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Range presentation and supporting analysis.","grader_hint":"Check that endpoints are ordered, units and included remedies are stable, and later summaries do not silently change the range."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The range is presented as an uncertainty-based planning estimate rather than as a prediction, adjudicated damages figure or guaranteed settlement value.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Qualifications accompanying the range.","grader_hint":"The language should preserve uncertainty without becoming so vague that the range is unusable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"If the memorandum displays the prior 10%-of-revenue shortcut against 2024 revenue, the calculation equals 10% of $13.4 million, subject only to reasonable rounding.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule-of-thumb comparison.","grader_hint":"The calculated benchmark is $1.34m. Award if the shortcut is discussed without a current calculation only when no conflicting amount is given."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states when a revenue-percentage rule of thumb stops being useful, including when claim posture, remedy type or exposure drivers are not correlated with revenue.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitation stated alongside the shortcut.","grader_hint":"The discussion must identify conditions, not merely call the shortcut crude."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains why the shortcut is particularly unreliable here, including the absence of an asserted matter and the changed product-specific risk profile.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Application of the rule-of-thumb limitation to the client.","grader_hint":"Look for client-specific reasoning rather than a generic warning about percentages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparable set uses defensible inclusion criteria based on children’s digital products, privacy or consent conduct, resolution posture, remedy and scale.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable-selection explanation or table fields.","grader_hint":"The criteria need not be formulaic, but the reader should understand why each primary comparable is included and why outliers receive less weight."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Outcomes outside the stated four-year window are not used as primary numerical comparables; any older matter is clearly labelled as background only.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dates and roles assigned to cited matters.","grader_hint":"Inspect every matter materially used to derive the range."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The regulatory set is materially complete for prominent 2021–2024 U.S. children’s digital-product outcomes that bear on the analysis, rather than relying on one convenient headline.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable table and narrative.","grader_hint":"Expect appropriate treatment of Kurbo/WW International, Epic Games, Edmodo, Microsoft/Xbox, NGL Labs and Tilting Point, allowing omission only with a reasoned scope distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Kurbo/WW International matter is correctly characterised as a 2022 regulatory resolution involving a $1.5 million civil penalty and data-deletion or algorithmic-remedy features, not a private class recovery.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and remedy classification.","grader_hint":"Equivalent accurate naming and concise characterisation qualify."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Epic Games outcome keeps the $275 million civil penalty separate from the $245 million refund component and treats Epic’s scale as an outlier limitation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and use in range derivation.","grader_hint":"Do not award if the two amounts are collapsed into an undifferentiated penalty or transplanted mechanically to the client."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Microsoft/Xbox matter is accurately identified as a 2023 regulatory resolution with a $20 million civil penalty and is qualified for platform-scale differences.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and weighting.","grader_hint":"Check year, amount, remedy type and treatment of scale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Edmodo matter distinguishes the announced $6 million civil penalty from the suspended payment treatment based on inability to pay.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and payable-value analysis.","grader_hint":"The memorandum must not use $6m as though it were an ordinary cash payment without the suspension qualification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The NGL Labs resolution correctly separates the approximately $4.5 million consumer-redress component from the $500,000 civil penalty within the $5 million headline amount.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and remedy taxonomy.","grader_hint":"Reasonable rounding and equivalent terminology are acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Tilting Point matter is accurately treated as a 2024 children’s-privacy regulatory resolution with a $500,000 civil penalty, with nonmonetary relief not converted into an invented cash amount.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable entry and range treatment.","grader_hint":"Check year, amount and separation of monetary from injunctive terms."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Adjacent matters involving devices, platforms or substantially larger enterprises are labelled as analogues or outliers rather than silently presented as same-scale app-publisher comparables.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable hierarchy and caveats.","grader_hint":"Examples may include voice assistants, consoles or major game platforms; judge the quality of the scope distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Settled private actions are treated accurately: supported final settlements may be used with their fund, fee and claims limitations, while the absence of a reliable same-window class analogue is disclosed rather than filled with allegations, verdicts or proposed settlements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Private-action section and cited procedural postures.","grader_hint":"Either a well-supported settled private comparator or a candid finding that none is sufficiently comparable can earn full credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The analysis maintains a meaningful taxonomy among civil penalties, consumer refunds or redress, disgorgement, class settlement funds, fees and nonmonetary relief.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels in the comparable analysis and range derivation.","grader_hint":"Different remedies need not occupy separate tables, but they must not be treated as interchangeable cash measures."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Suspended or conditional amounts are separately identified and are not treated as cash paid without qualification.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment of suspended resolutions.","grader_hint":"Check both matter summaries and any calculations that use them."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes announced headline amounts from amounts actually payable, funded or distributed where public information supports that distinction.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparable values and explanatory notes.","grader_hint":"Award when the distinction affects weighting or uncertainty rather than appearing only as boilerplate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any per-user metric uses a disclosed, relevant denominator and does not interchange total users, self-declared under-13 users, child profiles and accounts lacking consent evidence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Per-user calculations and denominator definitions.","grader_hint":"Recalculate any displayed metric and verify that unlike populations are not silently substituted."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The range methodology does not blend unlike remedy categories or incomparable enterprise-scale outcomes into a single unqualified average.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Range derivation and weighting approach.","grader_hint":"A qualitative bracket or scenario method is acceptable; a raw mean is acceptable only if comparability and category problems are adequately controlled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Comparable weighting considers material drivers beyond user count, such as duration, knowledge, monetisation, cooperation, remediation, ability to pay and enterprise scale, without claiming that all were established for the client.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Adjustment-factor discussion.","grader_hint":"Award for a disciplined selection of relevant factors; the memorandum need not speculate about unknown client facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Injunctive, deletion, monitoring and compliance obligations are discussed separately from monetary recovery and are not assigned invented dollar values.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment of nonmonetary relief.","grader_hint":"Qualitative recognition of operational significance is sufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum does not describe allegations, complaints or consent-order recitals as adjudicated client-equivalent violations or admissions of liability.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Procedural descriptions and analogical reasoning.","grader_hint":"Look for accurate qualifiers such as alleged, settled, stipulated or without adjudication where appropriate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document explains that a consent order or settlement amount is evidence of a negotiated resolution, not binding damages precedent or a tariff automatically applicable per user.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Doctrinal limitation on comparable use.","grader_hint":"The limitation must be applied to the valuation, not merely recited."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recognition analysis states that accounting accrual requires the applicable likelihood threshold and reasonable estimability, and it does not claim that a calculable exposure range alone compels booking.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Loss-contingency doctrine and conclusion.","grader_hint":"Equivalent professional terminology is acceptable; assess the substance rather than demanding a particular citation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Because no claim or investigation has been asserted, the memorandum separately considers whether assertion of a claim is sufficiently likely before treating an unfavourable outcome as an accrual question.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unasserted-claim analysis.","grader_hint":"The document must not skip directly from internal issues to a booked settlement estimate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum draws the disclosure boundary between accrued, reasonably possible and remote exposure without claiming that every non-accrued matter needs no disclosure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Accounting-treatment discussion.","grader_hint":"Award when recognition and disclosure are treated as distinct decisions and the wording avoids categorical overstatement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document expressly separates what must follow from the accounting recognition analysis from what should be maintained for prudent planning, negotiation or remediation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Must/should boundary in the recommendation.","grader_hint":"The distinction should determine how the booked amount and exposure range are labelled and used."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Probability of an asserted and unfavourable outcome is analysed separately from potential magnitude; a severe possible outcome is not treated as necessarily probable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk framework and conclusions.","grader_hint":"Look for two separate analytical dimensions and no multiplication presented as established fact without support."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The numerical recommendation and range avoid false precision and make material assumptions, inclusions and exclusions understandable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rounding, labels and methodology notes.","grader_hint":"Ranges rounded to a defensible level are preferable to unsupported dollar-level precision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum identifies concrete actions that could materially reduce uncertainty before or during the audit, including validation of consent records and the mixed-audience consent flow.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action or dependency discussion.","grader_hint":"Actions must respond to the supplied risk facts rather than being generic litigation-preservation boilerplate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Public matters are identified with enough source information to verify the authority, date, parties, posture and monetary components offline from the citation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Footnotes, endnotes, parentheticals or compact source list.","grader_hint":"URLs are not required. Official agency release or order titles and dates generally suffice; unsupported press shorthand does not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The five-page work product is professionally organised, legible and concise enough for counsel and auditors to use without asking the author to reconcile terminology or figures.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document review.","grader_hint":"Consider hierarchy, table readability, prose economy, defined terms and whether the decision can be located quickly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Insurance recovery, indemnification, tax treatment and deductibility are not assumed; if mentioned, they are identified as unknown and kept separate from gross exposure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Range assumptions and treatment of offsets.","grader_hint":"Award if the topics are omitted or responsibly qualified, provided no unsupported netting occurs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The range avoids double counting overlapping remedies or populations, including penalties plus embedded headline totals and overlapping child-account denominators.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Calculation trace and range construction.","grader_hint":"Reconstruct the range sufficiently to determine whether the same amount or population enters twice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any recommended audit follow-up has a concrete owner or decision maker and timing tied to the 13 January 2025 auditor arrival.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action assignments and timing.","grader_hint":"The owner may be a named supplied person, counsel, finance or the product unit; do not require invented employee names."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum ends on the single accounting decision or an unmistakable restatement of it, rather than ending with an unresolved next-steps list.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final substantive paragraph or decision box.","grader_hint":"A short action list may precede the ending, but the last substantive takeaway must be the recommended recorded amount and its status."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Meadowgate Advisory is advising Saltmarsh Energy on the year-end treatment of potential children’s-app privacy exposure. Counsel must give the CFO and auditors a decision-ready accounting recommendation without presenting a planning estimate as an established legal liability.","difficulty_sources":["Material ambiguity in the word reserve between an accounting balance and a management exposure estimate","Application of loss-contingency doctrine to potential but unasserted claims","Selection of a materially complete comparable set from professional knowledge without supplied sources","Separation of civil penalties, suspended amounts, redress, class funds and nonmonetary relief","Scaling highly visible but dissimilar enterprise outcomes to a smaller portfolio without false precision","Drawing an unobvious boundary between mandatory accounting treatment and prudent planning","Replacing an attractive revenue-percentage shortcut with a defensible, traceable range"],"authoring_plan":{"mode":"doctrinal","solver_instance":"An experienced commercial or privacy lawyer preparing a short annual loss-contingency memorandum for counsel, finance and auditors.","behaviour_specification":["Choose deliberately whether the document primarily uses reserve as the accounting balance or as management exposure, acknowledge the competing usage, and keep the two outputs distinct.","Make one accounting recommendation while separately supplying a gross planning range.","Apply loss-contingency doctrine to an unasserted matter: consider likelihood of assertion before likelihood of an unfavourable outcome, then estimability and disclosure.","Treat publicly announced settlements as negotiated outcomes rather than damages precedent and distinguish remedy types and procedural postures.","Use the client’s changed product facts to explain why a flat revenue percentage is unreliable in this setting."],"imperfections_and_tensions_to_preserve":["The prior-period reserve was generated by a convenient revenue percentage that produces an arithmetically clean answer but is not causally aligned with the current exposure.","The word reserve has two professionally plausible meanings for the intended readers.","No claim, regulator contact or investigation is asserted, while the internal facts show a materially worsening risk profile.","Public outcomes use headline values that combine penalties, refunds, suspended amounts and nonmonetary relief.","The most visible outcomes involve enterprises much larger than the client, while closer-scale matters may have suspended or modest monetary terms.","Private class outcomes within the fixed window may be sparse or procedurally unsuitable, so completeness does not mean filling the category with allegations or proposed settlements.","User populations are not interchangeable: total users, self-declared children, child profiles and accounts lacking retrievable consent evidence must remain distinct."],"gold_relationships":["Prior portfolio revenue equals the sum of the three prior-period product revenues, and the prior booked amount equals 10% of that total.","Current portfolio revenue equals the sum of the three current-period product revenues; any displayed 10% benchmark must use that sum.","The accounting recommendation must be a single point amount and must follow the author’s stated likelihood, assertion and estimability conclusions.","The planning range must have ordered endpoints, a stable remedy scope and no duplicated components.","A headline that combines a penalty and redress must be disaggregated before comparison.","A suspended penalty cannot be treated as ordinary cash paid.","Any per-user value must divide a relevant monetary component by a disclosed affected-user population and cannot be transferred across incompatible populations without qualification.","Nonmonetary relief may affect qualitative severity but receives no invented cash value.","The largest platform outcomes may inform scenario ceilings or remedy structure but cannot control the range without scale adjustment.","Recognition, disclosure and prudent management planning are separate conclusions and must not be represented as automatic substitutes for one another."],"doctrinal_rule_and_nonclaim_pairs":[{"rule":"Accrual depends on the applicable likelihood threshold and reasonable estimability.","must_not_claim":"A calculable exposure range, internal control weakness or serious possible outcome automatically requires booking."},{"rule":"For an unasserted matter, likelihood of assertion must be considered before an unfavourable-outcome conclusion is used for accrual.","must_not_claim":"Known compliance gaps are equivalent to a filed action or announced investigation."},{"rule":"Non-accrual and disclosure are separate decisions.","must_not_claim":"If no amount is booked, no disclosure can be appropriate."},{"rule":"Consent orders and settlements provide negotiated comparison points.","must_not_claim":"They adjudicate liability, establish binding per-user tariffs or prove the same result for the client."},{"rule":"Remedy components must be classified before comparison.","must_not_claim":"Civil penalties, refunds, disgorgement, class funds, fees, suspended amounts and compliance costs are interchangeable."},{"rule":"Planning ranges may support prudent management action even when accounting recognition is not met.","must_not_claim":"The planning range is itself the required booked balance."}],"accepted_verdicts":["A booking-centered memorandum may recommend zero or a nominal recorded amount because no matter has been asserted and the probability and estimability predicates are not sufficiently established, while separately giving a nonzero planning range and addressing disclosure.","An exposure-centered memorandum may lead with a defensible gross range but must still state one accounting amount and show why that amount does or does not satisfy the recognition threshold.","A nonzero accounting recommendation can pass if it expressly and defensibly concludes that assertion and loss are sufficiently likely, supports reasonable estimability without false precision, and does not merely roll forward the revenue shortcut."],"reference_solution_validation":["Render the DOCX through a standard offline office suite and confirm a maximum of five pages.","Recalculate portfolio totals, percentage benchmarks, every per-user metric and all range components.","Verify that matter amounts are separated by remedy type and that suspended portions are not treated as paid.","Trace the recommended accounting amount and range endpoints through every appearance.","Have both reviewers test the memorandum against all rubric items and resolve any factual or doctrinal disagreement before marking the gold as passed."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"reserve_memo","filename":"saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"Decision memorandum to counsel comparing the two periods, assessing the litigation-reserve treatment, and recommending a recorded amount with a separately reasoned exposure range.","required":true,"constraints":["A valid DOCX of no more than five rendered pages, placed at the top level of the working directory under the exact filename. It must use an information cutoff of 31 December 2024 and must not invent client facts or public-matter amounts."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"lawyers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":6.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution must be prepared by a lawyer experienced in privacy enforcement and loss-contingency memoranda, then independently reviewed by a second domain reviewer for accounting-boundary accuracy, public-matter characterisation, arithmetic, internal consistency and five-page usability. Reviewers must accept either a booking-centered or exposure-centered primary interpretation if the memorandum defines it deliberately, still supplies one accounting recommendation, and maintains the doctrinal distinction throughout.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No live research or supplied source packet is available. The gold therefore uses a fixed information cutoff of 31 December 2024 and a stable core of prominent public outcomes that domain reviewers can verify from established official materials. The precise planning range is judgment-dependent, so grading focuses on traceable remedy classification, disciplined comparability and consistency rather than one literal range. A zero or nominal booked amount may be defensible given the absence of an asserted matter, but it is not mandated if a submission supplies a professionally supported alternative application of the recognition threshold."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:07:32+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:07:32+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:07:32+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:07:32+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R05']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:07:32+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/163825ded2be6d83","declared_deliverables":["saltmarsh_litigation_reserve_memo.docx"]};
